## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account data governance at scale beyond single-service configs.

## Key Concepts

- Lake Formation cross-account data sharing
- IAM roles with trusted principals and external IDs
- RAM for resource sharing
- KMS cross-account encryption
- EventBridge/Glue orchestration
- Auditing and revocation testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: assume cross-account role and write a marker to shared S3
const { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-sts");
const { S3Client, PutObjectCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-s3");

async function testAccess(roleArn, bucket, key, body) {
  const sts = new STSClient({});
  const r = await sts.send(new AssumeRoleCommand({ RoleArn: roleArn, RoleSessionName: "AuditTest" }));
  const s3 = new S3Client({ credentials: {
    accessKeyId: r.Credentials.AccessKeyId,
    secretAccessKey: r.Credentials.SecretAccessKey,
    sessionToken: r.Credentials.SessionToken
  }});
  await s3.send(new PutObjectCommand({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key, Body: body }));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test and enforce revocation across all accounts?
- How would you verify no data exposure in an incident scenario across accounts?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses capability to design tenant isolation, data residency, and governance at scale using AWS services.

## Key Concepts
- Region-bound storage for residency compliance
- Fine-grained access with Lake Formation and IAM conditions
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant CMKs
- Automated onboarding/offboarding and disaster recovery

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
